sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch04-02> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "The spindle and asters of dividing cells are lines of force of a bipolar field made visible by more permeable particles, like iron filings between magnet poles, and they cannot be explained by mere mechanical traction on a protoplasmic web but require some polarised and polarising force analogous to magnetism or electricity.";
    dc:type "mechanism";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Mitotic figures require a polarised force like magnetism, not mechanical traction";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter IV, “On the Internal Form and Structure of the Cell”; topic “Spindle and asters as lines of force”. Scope: caryokinetic (mitotic) division of animal and plant cells"
}
